  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Infrastructure</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Financed the construction of a safe space in Dili for victims of Gender-Based Violence (GBV). It was built following Ministry of Health (MoH) guidelines, on which PHD was consulted, incorporating international infrastructure minimum standards from the United Nations Population Fund (UNFPA). The safe space has improved access to confidential GBV services, leading to increased GBV reporting, which has enhanced our ability to identify at-risk groups. PHD&apos;s advocacy has prompted the Ministry of Health, for the first time, to allocate budget in 2024 for the construction of safe spaces. These spaces are under construction using recently MoH-approved, PHD-funded guidelines.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Marie Stopes, through DFAT&apos;s Timor-Leste Human Development Program, generated 55,000 Couple-Years of Protection (CYP) in the reporting period and made significant strides in strengthening Timor-Leste&#x2019;s Ministry of Health (MoH) to deliver sexual and reproductive health services including family planning services in two municipalities. Australia&apos;s support has trained MoH healthcare providers to independently deliver these services and averted an estimated 27,901 unintended pregnancies.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">PHD continued to build the partnership between Organisations of Persons with Disabilities (OPDs), RHTO and ADTL, and the Ministry of Education (MoE), particularly the Department of Inclusive Education and links to the Educational Management Information System. Joint technical support was provided to MoE for the development of the Child Functioning Screening Tool (CFST) to help identify children with functional difficulties.  
&#x200d;
MoE adopted the tool through a ministerial dispatch mandating the nationwide use of the CFST as an integral component of the new school enrolment form. The enrolment form has now been used in December 2024 and January 2025 during student registration across Timor-Leste. 
&#x200d;
To support the rollout of the CFST, PHD worked in close coordination with OPDs who conducted awareness raising in schools and for parents and provided referral support at the school level.  The enrolment form has now been used in December 2024 and January 2025 during student registration across Timor-Leste. 
&#x200d;
Additionally, in 20 pilot schools in Baucau and Lautem, teachers have used the tool to help identify existing students with functional difficulties and implement more inclusive teaching approaches, for example, flexible seating arrangements to support mobility and visibility and peer support.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">PHD facilitated a series of consultations in partnership with the National Institute for Public Health (INSPTL) which resulted in the development of their training strategy, including the quality assurance framework for in-service training.  Whilst final review and approval are underway, INSPTL and Maluk Timor are already utilising the quality assurance framework as a guiding document to develop the integrated clinical and non-clinical in-services training modules for primary health care.  The quality assurance framework provides clear guidance for competency-based training modules and the monitoring of in-services training effectiveness.
&#x200d;
INSPTL will continue to utilise the two documents to address the essential gaps in training governance, such as the lack of mechanisms for selection, certification, remuneration and performance monitoring of its trainers and the cost-effective approach for incentivizing health workers for continued professional development.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Disability equity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">PHD supported Ra&#x2019;es Hadomi Timor Oan (RHTO) and Associa&#xe7;&#xe3;o Defici&#xea;nsia Timor-Leste (ADTL) and 11 OPDs sub-grantees with financial support, engagement with relevant line ministries, and policy analysis mentoring and training to achieve the following:
&#x200d;
RHTO
&#x200d;
-Collaborated with the Ministry of Education (MoE) to assess accessibility in four municipalities and supported planning for 2026 budget allocations.
&#x200d;
-Contributed to infrastructure design analysis with the Ministry of Public Works.
&#x200d;
-Engaged by MoH and MoE to integrate OPD insights into accessible infrastructure design.
&#x200d;
-Contributed to improved access to public services through accurate identifications of people with disability, and support for essential documentation and referrals. This enabled 120 children with disability to access education, 105 people with disability to access civic documentation and rehabilitation, and 30 to access university
&#x200d;
ADTL 
&#x200d;
-Led the consultative process to develop, collect data for and co-author Timor-Leste&#x2019;s first UN Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities (CRPD) alternative report, involving 21 disability organisations and 55 individuals with disabilities.
&#x200d;
-Established and managed its first small grants program (AUD 90,654.42), supporting 11 disability organisations to benefit over 200 people with disabilities.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">PHD&#x2019;s technical support to the Ministry of Health (MoH), specifically the National Institute for Pharmacies and Medical Products (INFPM) has improved medical supplies inventory management through the mSupply system at the central warehouse in Dili. These improvements have resulted in a clear categorisation of vital, essential, necessary and special medicines and a real-time mechanism for monitoring levels of stock. Online requisition through mSupply has allowed health facilities to track orders, reduce the incidence of incorrect product deliveries and prevent oversupply of medical supplies. 
&#x200d;
PHD&#x2019;s support to facilitate RHTO, an Organisation of Persons with Disabilities (OPD), to provide technical inputs to MoH&#x2019;s health facilities&#x2019; infrastructure improvement work has improved accessibility for people with physical disability, to Community Health Centres (CHCs) in Comoro, Gleno, Atsabe, and Passabe, in Dili, Ermera, and Oecusse. PHD&#x2019;s facilitation has ensured accessibility standards, such as ramps, handrails, and accessible WASH facilities are integrated into new health facility designs and existing infrastructure assessments, enhancing MoH engineers&#x2019; understanding of inclusive health infrastructure. PHD also engaged RHTO officials to join MoH engineers&apos; team in the pre and post assessment of health facility infrastructure improvement work.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Disability equity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Timor-Leste Child Functioning Screening Tool (CFST) developed in 2019 by the Timor-Lese Ministry of Education, with support from PHD, UNICEF and inputs from organisations of people with disabilities (OPDs) is enabling identification of children with functional difficulties in primary schools. During 2023-2024, with PHD support the CFST and associated training program has been implemented across 20 public schools in two municipalities. PHD trained School Inclusive Education Focal Points are undertaking pre-screening of children in selected grades and identified 349 students experiencing functional difficulties. Teachers have been trained to use the screening results to facilitate more inclusive training practices to improve students&#x2019; learning outcomes, and school leaders have observed and documented the increasing use of inclusive practices among teachers. OPDs&#x2019; participation throughout the process and as observers in training teachers on the importance of inclusive education has likely been crucial for adoption of improved teaching practices.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Through mobilisation of a rapid multisectoral health emergency response aided by the Australian Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade (DFAT) and the Partnership for Human Development (PHD), Timor-Leste&#x2019;s Ministry of Health (MoH) was able to control and reduce the further spread of rabies. PHD and DFAT supported MoH in the roll-out of the response, including though the procurement and supply of rabies vaccines, Rabies Immune Globulin (RIG) and rabies diagnostic tests for rabies detection in humans. Without coordinated support from DFAT and PHD the rabies outbreak would likely be more widespread and have resulted in more human fatalities.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Timor-Leste&apos;s Ministry of Social Solidarity and Inclusion (MSSI), with support from the Partnership for Human Development (PHD), has significantly improved efficiency of a social assistance payment program. PHD recruited a Public Financial Management Adviser to streamline bureaucratic processes; engaged Payment Service Providers for faster payments; and developed a digital Management Information System to enhance payment recording and verification. PHD support improved MSSI capacity to fulfil its mandate by reducing payment processing time from 83 working days in 2022 to just 32 working days in 2023, enabling more timely payments to pregnant women and children from birth until 5 years of age. The Government of Timor-Leste made the decision to discontinue the program. However, lessons have been captured for future Social Protection programming.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Social protection measures</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Timor-Leste&apos;s Ministry of Social Solidarity and Inclusion (MSSI), with support from the Partnership for Human Development (PHD), improved the efficiency of the Bolsa da Mae &#x2013; Jerasaun Foun, social assistance payment program. PHD recruited a Public Financial Management Adviser to streamline bureaucratic processes; engaged Payment Service Providers for faster payments; and developed a digital Management Information System to enhance payment recording and verification. PHD support improved MSSI capacity to fulfil its mandate by reducing payment processing time from 83 working days in 2022 to just 32 working days in 2023. As a result, as at the end of December 2023 more than 59, 334 beneficiaries (including 40, 670 payees) received payments across 7 municipalities.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Upon confirmation of the first rabies case in Oecusse, PHD, in collaboration with the Menzies School of Health Research, PHD supported the Ministry of Health (MoH) to develop guidelines for responding to dog bites, including pre- and post-exposure rabies prophylaxis. This was followed by training, health education and rabies advocacy activities delivered to high-risk border areas to strengthen the existing mechanism for integrated disease surveillance, response and mitigation. In addition, training on the spatial analysis of rabies cases using Quantum GIS software was delivered, building capacity within the National Institute for Public Health (INSPTL) for disease surveillance. PHD also provided technical and coordination support to Municipal Health Services on the implementation of the Dog Bite Guidelines. 
&#x200d;
PHD has also supported the procurement of rabies vaccines, reagents, and Rabies Immunoglobulin (RIG) enabling MoH&#x2019;s response to existing cases while mobilising resources to curb the spread. The procurement of RIG and vaccine, with The National Institute for Pharmaceutical and Medical Products, was used to successfully test the mSupply workflow, enabling stock tracking and timely coordination with partners for future procurement. 
&#x200d;
This hybrid approach, strengthening systems while ensuring vaccines and prophylaxis are available, has strengthened MoH&apos;s response to the spread of rabies in Timor-Leste.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
